        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Kingen',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Kingen (also known as King) is a Belgian and French trick-taking game played by four individuals. It is a 'compendium' game where a full session consists of a series of rounds with different objectivesâ€”some rounds are 'negative' (avoiding specific tricks or cards) while others are 'positive' (trying to win tricks). It is the European parent of the game Barbu.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dates to the early 20th century in Western Europe. It evolved from simpler 'penalty' games like Hearts into a structured multi-round tournament format favored by university students and social clubs.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players playing individually.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Aces are high, 2s are low.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Aces are high, 2s are low.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player receives 13 cards. The role of dealer rotates clockwise.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
